  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nLatest GOES Water Vapor imagery shows an upper level low (ULL) \nslowly approaching from the Great Lakes centered over southern \nOntario. This low will continue to track east-southeastward \nthroughout the day tracking from western New York this morning \nto the mid- Hudson Valley this evening into tonight then off \nthe New England coast by Sunday morning. Ahead of this system, \npartly to mostly cloudy conditions are expected for the rest of \nthe night with some rain showers overspreading areas west of the\nHudson Valley as the ULL nears. Some fog, locally dense, will \noccur through the rest of the night for portions of the mid- \nHudson Valley and Litchfield Hills into the Berkshires. Lows \nwill be mainly in the 40s to near 50.\n\nThe track of the ULL combined with the presence of an inverted \ntrough and strong divergent flow aloft will favor the greatest \nshower activity and potential for convective downpours for areas\nalong and north of Interstate 90. A pivoting band of showers is\nlikely to occur but exactly where it sets up remains uncertain,\nif it occurs across northern areas or just north of our CWA. If\nthe pivoting occurs in northern areas, a general 0.25 to 0.75 \ninches of rain will be possible with some locally higher amounts\nover 1 inch. Even with these possible higher amounts, no \nhydrological impacts are expected. Lesser rainfall amounts are \nexpected for areas south of I-90.\n\nShowers will continue tonight as the ULL begins to depart and\nlinger into Sunday morning, especially for areas outside of the\nHudson Valley, due to west to northwesterly moist, upslope \nflow. Drier weather briefly returns for Sunday afternoon and \nevening before another disturbance approaches for Sunday night \ninto early Monday.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\nNear to slightly above normal temperatures are expected for\nearly to mid next week. Additional disturbances will pass\nthrough the area at times bringing some additional light\nshowers. The first arrives late Sunday night through Monday.\nIf showers linger long enough into later Monday afternoon or\nevening, a few snowflakes may mix in across some of the highest\nelevations of the Adirondacks and eastern Catskills but will\nlikely lead to no accumulation. Additional disturbances pass to\nthe north on Tuesday and Wednesday which may bring a few showers\nmainly to areas north of I-90. Longer range guidance is in \nagreement on a large upper trough/upper level low to form over \nthe eastern CONUS later next week into next weekend. The overall\nplacement and orientation of this upper trough/low remains \nhighly uncertain. This will favor a return to below normal \ntemperatures for the end of the week into next weekend. This may\nalso allow a surface low pressure system to form and bring \nadditional precipitation to the area. With the cooler\ntemperatures, will monitor the potential for some precipitation\nto be in the form of snow, especially for the higher \nelevations.",
